rota lap 4550, row 2 -- Fire, the row that sees: cut and stop. Advanced by hand from row 0, which lap 4550 mod 5 names and which nine of today's shelf rows had already read; Fire was the least-read at six. Standfast, reds-first, and the three depths read whole. What it surfaced: the thing that had to be cut had already written its own indictment, in its own header, and left it as a comment.
think The cold open read 234 guards, 224 green, 8 red, tree_moved=no, head_behind_anointed=0. standing_equipment is the self-read of the other seven, so seven stood. I sorted them by whether a lap of mine could close one on metal.
think fold_shelf_link and fold_shelf_link_repoint were one fault seen twice: a single depth-lost link in construction/archive/20260910-070937_itinerary-landed-accounts.md, naming archive/20260910-053839_... from inside construction/archive/. The scan had already computed the correction and the repointer's own witness names the repair in its refusal text, so this was typing rather than judgment. sh tools/fixtures/f/fold_shelf_link_repoint.sh wrote one edit; both guards green.
think tame_reach red at uncovered_authored=553 against a ceiling of 552 -- over by ONE. The 604 in the transcript is the uncovered count including 51 fixture plants, which is not the gated reading.
think The scan's own header, written 20260909.002917, names the braid exactly: uncovered_authored answers how much debt stands, which only falls, and how many such files exist, which rises whenever anyone writes one. It raised the ceiling 551 -> 552 for tools/rye/perf_self_count.rye, booked the split by stamp, and left it because REDS had no room for a row.
think I found the second firing by dating every uncovered authored file with git log --diff-filter=A: tools/rye/wrap_ring.rye, 20260910.061931, a moonshot witness landing in tools/rye, a room already uncovered. Lawful work, refused by a gate. A lantern that fires twice becomes a loom.
think The reading that can keep the witness's own promise is a top-level ROOM rather than a file, because a room is what a widening moves and its files move with it. tools/fixtures/t/tame_reach_debt_rooms.txt names the 25 rooms holding uncovered authored Rye; uncovered_rooms_unknown is gated at zero against it, debt_rooms ratchets at 25, and uncovered_authored is printed with uncovered_authored_gated=no.
obs The witness had claimed the file ceiling bought "a room born tomorrow joins the roster, or this guard hears it on the lap it arrives." It never could: a new room's first .rye raises the count by exactly one, the same as a new file in an old room, so the two cases were indistinguishable and the gate red on both. Control legs 11 and 12 are those two files side by side -- one leaves every gate alone, one refuses -- which is the argument stated as a measurement.
obs Legs 11 and 12 had to move to the END of the control. Written inline at section 2 they added a file to the pen and shifted six later expected counts by one, which read as six regressions in a pen that was correct. A leg that grows a population belongs after every leg that counts it.
obs The absent-debt-list leg failed first because it named no roster, so the scan refused on the roster before it could reach the debt list. Both refusals are real and only the second was under test; the leg now sets both.
obs uncovered_authored_gated=no is a key rather than a comment on purpose. The elder gate is retired, and a reader meeting a number with no ceiling beside it cannot tell "held by nothing" from "held somewhere I have not looked" -- which is Gauge's fifth thing a figure must name.
obs unwatched_ban_hits carries the same braid: a new uncovered file with a ban raises it too. It is green at 736 today, so I named the shape in the scan header rather than widening this lap.
obs unheard_guard is my own class one room over -- unnamed_population=460, moved by hand six times, each move carrying a paragraph checking the delta from both sides. That is deliberate and careful rather than wrong, so I named it for Keaton rather than rewriting a peer's instrument.
obs No REDS row could land: construction/REDS.md reads 40,954 of 40,960, six bytes. The finding is named on the card, as the two laps before mine also had to.
obs The rebase onto xy/main dropped my fold_shelf_link repoint: BAKERY repaired the same one link the same hour, so upstream already carried it and git kept theirs. Two ships reading one red and reaching for the same proven tool is the tool working; the cost was one duplicated lap-minute, which is cheaper than either of us leaving it.
obs The hot pass also read geode_libraries red -- docs-geode/libraries/README.md claiming crypto at 121 witnesses where the tree holds 120, a peer's page left unregenerated. I regenerated it while the pass ran, which is exactly the tree_moved=yes the card warns against, so that pass is void and the figures I claim are a second one run on the settled tree.
obs The card would not hold this account at its natural length. Upstream stood at 40,929 of 40,960 with eight ships on the live front, so my block had to come in within 31 bytes of the 22-line one it replaced; it took nine trimming passes to land at 40,955. That pressure is real and it is Keaton's raise to make, not mine.
